{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Arizona Diamondbacks arrived with expectations and delivered a 93-win season that ended the franchise's long postseason drought. From the opening days, the club showed it could score in bunches — 812 runs on the season, fueled by 220 home runs — and a pitching staff with a 3.66 ERA that consistently kept them in games. The early months of April and May were promising, as the team built a double-digit game advantage through a blend of timely hitting and bullpen resilience, even as individual costly moments reminded them how thin the margins of baseball can be.\n\nJune continued the momentum with a 17-9 record, one of the best months in the NL West, though Zack Greinke took some lumps along the way and the bullpen occasionally wavered. The July swoon — a 10-14 skid — was the low point, a stretch where the offense went quiet and the relief corps struggled to protect leads, with Fernando Rodney and others allowing crucial late-game runs. It was the one moment when the playoff dream seemed genuinely at risk, the desert heat of summer testing the team's resolve.\n\nAugust and September proved the Diamondbacks were built for the long haul. They went 32-24 over those two months, with Paul Goldschmidt anchoring the offense and the rotation stabilizing. A grand slam from Jake Lamb that backfired in one of the summer's cruelest moments was answered by clutch contributions from unexpected sources — Fernando Rodney delivering a wild-card win and Archie Bradley providing one of the postseason's most memorable moments with a bases-clearing triple in the Wild Card game against Colorado.\n\nThe October run was brief but electric. The Wild Card victory over the Rockies sent Chase Field into a frenzy, with Bradley's pinch-hit triple cementing cult-hero status. But the NLDS against the dynastic Dodgers proved a bridge too far. Taijuan Walker surrendered a key three-run homer to Justin Turner in Game 1, and despite Paul Goldschmidt and Daniel Descalso providing late-game heroics in Game 3, the Dodgers' depth and firepower proved overwhelming. The D-backs fell in three games, their season ending with a double play to close the book on an otherwise triumphant year.",
